| 1319 | T4 | 🔲 Open | Soon | P3 | `quality` | **Need browser-automation tool in agent session — visual regression sweep is fundamentally limited without one** (opened 2026-05-25 alongside #1318). Today the agent has to (a) start `vite dev` in the background, (b) launch headless Chrome via `chrome.exe --headless --screenshot=...`, (c) Read the PNG back and visually inspect. Workable but slow, no DOM inspection, no JS evaluation, no per-element bounding-rect dump. To close: install Playwright MCP or Chrome DevTools MCP in the user's `.mcp.json` so the agent can issue `page.goto`, `page.setViewportSize`, `page.evaluate(() => ...)`, and `page.screenshot` directly — that converts the #1310 backstop from "human pass required" to "agent can finish responsive sweeps end-to-end". Pairs with the existing pattern of running `npm run typecheck` as the static signal. |
| 1321 | T4 | 🔲 Open | Soon | P3 | `bug` | **PredictiveAssessments — `handlePickKind` shows a toast for anon users instead of routing through `<AuthOverlay>` with a snapshot of the picked kind** (opened 2026-05-25 alongside #1320). [PredictiveAssessments.tsx:106-110](hired.video/frontend/pages/tools/PredictiveAssessments.tsx) is the only gate seam left for this tool — anon users see the full catalog (good), then get a toast telling them to sign in (bad: no overlay, no return URL, no snapshot). To close: mirror the #1320 pattern — add `showAuthOverlay` state + a `usePendingToolInput<{ kind }>()` hook, snapshot the picked kind when unauth, render `<AuthOverlay contentType="tool" returnTo>` so the post-login redirect brings the user back to the same `/tools/predictive-assessments` URL and the picked kind auto-starts. Unblocks the same "see the page, click the CTA, then sign in" UX from #1320 for the assessment catalog. |
