| 511 | T1 | 🔲 Open | Soon | P2 | `feature` | **Gateway-internal retry billing semantics unspecified — risk of double-counting tokens against our user-side ledger** (2026-05-09) | The SDK response carries `_builderforce.retries: number` indicating how many times the gateway internally retried before returning a successful response. If the gateway charges the tenant key for each upstream attempt (typical pricing model), the `usage` we record from the final response captures all retries. We then debit that amount once on `tokenLedger.charge` — fine. BUT if the gateway returns 5xx after exhausting its internal retries AND we then advance to the NEXT profile in our cross-vendor chain (which the gateway doesn't know is a logically related call), the tenant has paid for both the failed retry burst AND the new profile's tokens. Our user-side ledger only sees the final successful charge. Result: tenant key spend > sum of user-side debits = gap. Fix: gateway documents whether `usage` on success includes retry tokens (yes/no), and whether failed-but-retried attempts are billed (yes/no). Builderforce SDK could expose `_builderforce.retryUsage[]` so our adapter can choose to debit each gateway retry separately. Until clarified: SA dashboard will under-report user-side spend vs tenant-key spend by the gateway-internal-retry delta. |
| 526 | T1 | 🔲 Open | Soon | P2 | `arch` | **Inquiry-notification push delivery still rides the deleted-from-FE VAPID system; subscription audience can only shrink** (2026-05-10) | Wired BurnRateOS push end-to-end on the FE side: page-side embed loader on `index.html`; `vite.config.ts` workbox `importScripts` injects BurnRateOS handlers into the auto-generated SW; deleted `lib/push-notifications.ts`, the push toggles in `Settings.tsx` (Lead Generation tab) + `ServicesManagement.tsx` (Add Service first-time opt-in), and the `/api/push/subscribe` + `/api/push/unsubscribe` routes (`api/src/routes/push.ts` removed, mount removed from `api/src/app.ts`). Still on the OLD VAPID path: (a) `sendPush()` helper + RFC 8030/8291/8292 JWT signer at [api/src/routes/services.ts:66-209](hired.video/api/src/routes/services.ts), (b) the two callers at [services.ts:560](hired.video/api/src/routes/services.ts#L560) (new service inquiry → notify provider) and [services.ts:607](hired.video/api/src/routes/services.ts#L607) (inquiry response → notify requester), (c) the `pushSubscriptions` Drizzle table in [api/src/db/schema/services.ts:63-74](hired.video/api/src/db/schema/services.ts#L63-L74) plus its `0002_services_marketplace.sql` migration. Existing rows still receive pushes; no new rows can be created (FE subscription path is gone), so the addressable audience monotonically shrinks toward zero. Fix in two passes: (1) port `sendPush()` to call BurnRateOS's REST send API keyed by `externalUserId = user.id` (same id the page-side embed registers); (2) once #1 ships and is validated in production, write a new drizzle migration `drop table push_subscriptions`, delete `pushSubscriptions` from the schema + `PushSubscription`/`NewPushSubscription` types, delete `sendPush()` + the VAPID JWT signer + the import in services.ts, drop `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` / `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` / `VAPID_SUBJECT` env secrets from Cloudflare. Unblocks: inquiry notifications reach the full user base (not just the legacy VAPID-subscribed cohort) and removes the only remaining dual-system push surface. |
| 535 | T1 | 🔲 Open | Soon | P2 | `arch` | **Email sync uses cron-poll (5min cadence), not Gmail Push / Outlook Graph subscriptions — sync latency floored at 5 minutes** (2026-05-10) | P1 shipped polling for simplicity (no Pub/Sub topic / Graph subscription lifecycle to manage). Trade-off: a candidate who applied 30s before a recruiter replies waits up to 5 minutes for the inbox row to appear. Real-time delivery requires: (Gmail) `users.watch` against a Pub/Sub topic the worker can subscribe to via webhook + 7-day renewal cron — `external_email_accounts.push_expires_at` + the `external_email_accounts_push_expiry_idx` index are already in place for the renewal query; (Outlook) Graph `/subscriptions` POST with a webhook URL + ~70min lifetime renewal cron. Webhook receivers would map `accountId` from the subscription resource and invoke `syncAccount(deps, account)` immediately. Unblocks: sub-minute inbox latency, lower API cost (Push fires only on change vs polling N accounts every 5min). |
| 739 | T4 | 🔲 Open | Soon | P2 | `quality` | **In-flight `useGatedResource` fetches aren't aborted when the user logs out — any request already over the wire at logout completes against the (now-cleared) token and surfaces as a 401 in the network panel** (2026-05-17 — surfaced while closing the inbox 401-after-logout report) | [use-gated-resource.ts](hired.video/frontend/hooks/use-gated-resource.ts) now re-checks `authService.isAuthenticated()` inside `refresh` so a polling tick that fires AFTER logout no longer hits the wire — covers the common race. Residual: a request that's already past that check and in `fetch()` can't be cancelled because [api-client.ts](hired.video/frontend/lib/api-client.ts) `RequestConfig` doesn't accept an external `AbortSignal` (the controller is internal-only, for timeouts). Fix: add optional `signal?: AbortSignal` to `RequestConfig`, plumb it through `executeRequest` (compose with the timeout controller via `AbortSignal.any`), then let `useGatedResource` own a controller per fetch and abort on `apiReady → false`. Unblocks: zero post-logout 401s across every hook built on the base (inbox, subscription, token budget, AI usage, comm hub, ...). |
