| 1180 | T1 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **Data export bundle is a single JSON file; should be a ZIP with one file per concern (profile.json + resumes.json + applications.json + messages.json + media.json + billing.json + README.md)** (opened 2026-05-24 alongside #1149 — renumbered from #1165 after sector-taxonomy + editorial work claimed lower IDs concurrently) | [routes/settings.ts](hired.video/api/src/routes/settings.ts) `/export` ships a single JSON bundle today — functional but unwieldy for large datasets (a user can't grab just their billing slice without parsing the whole tree) and not what most "download my data" patterns look like industry-wide (LinkedIn / Google / Facebook all ship ZIPs). To close: add a `jszip` (or CF-compatible alternative) dependency and switch the default to a multi-file ZIP. Keep the single-JSON output behind `?format=json-single` for tooling that parses it programmatically. Add a `README.md` to the ZIP root explaining the file layout + retention + how to file a deletion. Pairs with #1149. |
| 1246 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **DEFERRED v2 — Paid ticketing for events** (opened 2026-05-25 alongside #1240, explicit deferral). Stripe Connect Express onboarding, tiered tickets, promo codes, refund flow, payout dashboard, 1099 generation. Skipped at v1 launch because (a) PCI scope + refund-policy legal + state tax = feature-graveyard for new event platforms, (b) Luma + Eventbrite already own the paid segment and our differentiator (AI matchmaking + integrated networking + post-event gamification points) is orthogonal. When the data shows paid demand, copy Luma's instant-payout Stripe Connect model with a flat 5% fee that undercuts Eventbrite's 3.7% + $1.79 + 2.9%. Unblocks: pay-to-attend events, ticketed conferences. |
| 1472 | T1 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `quality` | **`api/src/services/__tests__/ai-billing-coverage.test.ts` heuristic could miss truly-unmetered call sites when systemPrompt + userMessage exceed 3000 chars** (opened 2026-05-25 during the test runner fix). The window-based detection now uses 3000 chars (was 600) to accommodate long template literals in [dashboard.ts](hired.video/api/src/routes/dashboard.ts) etc. The trade-off: if someone ships a route with a 4000-char prompt AND forgets `useCase:`, the test won't flag it. The right fix is an AST-based walker that finds the `callJson` call expression and inspects its sibling argument object's properties for `useCase`. Mid-priority — the regex caught the bug it was designed to catch (#428 leak), and the AST upgrade is a separate quality investment. |
