| 189 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **merge.dev unified ATS integration** | Complements existing per-provider `ats_connections` (#64) with merge.dev's unified API covering 60+ ATS systems (Bullhorn, Lever, Greenhouse, Workday, Workable, etc.). One OAuth flow on the agency side → bulk candidate import into `talent_pool_candidates` + `candidate_resumes`. Reduces custom provider integration work and enables faster agency onboarding. |
| 350 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **NLE: no automated quality control (QScan-equivalent)** | No black-frame detection, freeze-frame detection, audio silence / clipping / loudness-normalization checks, or compliance validation against delivery specs (broadcast loudness, aspect, codec). Fix: ship a pre-export `QcReport` panel that runs a frame sampler + audio meter against the rendered Timeline preview and lists violations against the chosen delivery profile. Unblocks: serious creator / broadcast workflows; differentiator vs CapCut / Canva. Identified 2026-05-05 during studio NLE audit. |
| 545 | T4 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `quality` | **Studio v2 A/B opt-in is localStorage-only — no server-side flag means we can't measure adoption or run a real A/B test** (2026-05-12) | [lib/studio/editor-v2/preferences.ts](hired.video/frontend/lib/studio/editor-v2/preferences.ts) stores the v2 opt-in in `localStorage["studio.editorVersion"]`. Pros: zero infra, no backend coupling. Cons: doesn't survive device-switch, doesn't reach analytics, and there's no event fired when users flip the toggle. To measure conversion (does v2 retention beat classic?) we need either (a) a `users.editor_preference` column + sync API call, OR (b) a reusable feature-flag/experiment primitive (no `useFeature()` hook exists yet — first call site would justify building one). Unblocks: data-driven decision to flip v2 to default. |
| 561 | T4 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `quality` | **Studio v2 themeId persists only via local autosave — reload from server loses the "active theme" highlight in the Design panel** (2026-05-13) | The CanvasState shape carries `themeId` (so the picker re-highlights on reload), but `canvasStateToTimeline` doesn't write it onto the Timeline JSON — only the visual effect (scene colours + widget colours) survives the round-trip. Fix: stash `themeId` in the first scene's `canvas-v2:background` effect params and read it on hydrate. Same channel as scene background colour, no schema change. Unblocks: design-tab UI fidelity across reloads. |
| 692 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **CHRO can't yet attach the user's voice clones / prior video clips to a generated draft** (2026-05-16 — surfaced while closing #688) | `resolveUserContent` in [resolve-user-content.ts](hired.video/frontend/lib/studio/editor-v2/resolve-user-content.ts) returns `{ profile, resume }` only. Two adjacent content sources that the AI CHRO conceptually owns aren't wired yet: (a) the user's voice clones from the Voice Clone Marketplace (would let `personal_brand` / `profile` drafts auto-attach a `VoiceoverConfig` referencing the user's chosen clone id), (b) the user's existing `studio_projects` whose final renders are in R2 (would let `sizzle_reel` auto-populate the empty Clip 1 / Clip 2 scenes from the user's top-3 most-viewed videos). Both are additive — extend `UserContentSnapshot` + add slot bindings that target the not-yet-existent video widget source + the project-level voiceover field. Unblocks: "make me a sizzle reel from my best videos" landing on a draft with real clips ready to trim. |
| 845 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **True broadcast-spec deliverables for TV Ads — BS.1770/EBU R128 loudness normalisation, CEA-608/708 sidecar captions, Rec.709 / BT.601 broadcast-safe gamut clamp, frame-perfect 6s/15s/20s/30s/60s slot locks** (opened 2026-05-19 — deferred half of the Studio aspect evaluation; user scoped current TV-Ad work to "looks like a TV ad" for social marketing teams, true broadcast specs roadmap) | Current Studio is being scoped to social-media TV-ad creators only — aspect presets + lower-thirds + end-cards + bug widget are in scope today. The broadcast-grade deliverables sit behind a separate render-path: (a) a `loudness-r128` normalisation pass in the offline mixer (target -23 LUFS short-term + -1 dBTP), (b) CEA-608/708 sidecar emit from existing caption shape so MP4 can carry closed captions in-band, (c) a `broadcast-safe-gamut` LUT that clamps to Rec.709 or BT.601 before encoding, (d) a "broadcast slot" preset family that LOCKS the project duration to 6.0/15.0/20.0/30.0/60.0s frame-perfect (no drift on import/export). Unblocks: agencies + brand teams who need to file actual TV deliverables; gates the "real" TV-Ad market segment. Out of scope for the current pass; would be a P0 the day we have a paying agency customer asking for it. |
| 846 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **Full custom 2D character rig of the user — bones / IK / blend shapes / lip-sync from script** (opened 2026-05-19 — deferred end-state of the Studio Characters direction; user scoped current work to Tier-A drop-in avatars, custom-self-rig as the roadmap goal) | Tier-A (stock avatar widget: Lottie / SVG loops dropped onto the canvas like any image) is the immediate scope. Tier-B (talking head: existing webcam + chroma-key + viseme-driven mouth shape from voiceover audio, ~2MB viseme model in the WASM bundle) is plausible mid-term. Tier-C (full 2D character rig of the user themselves: bone hierarchy, IK solver, blend shapes, automatic lip-sync from the script + voiceover, expression library, walk/talk/gesture loops) is the end-state user goal. Tier-C is genuinely out of architectural scope for an ffmpeg.wasm browser NLE — it needs a real animation runtime (Spine / Rive / DragonBones) embedded as a render layer plus a rig-from-photo pipeline (DensePose / mediapipe landmark → 2D rig binding). Recommended approach: keep Tier-C aspirational and ladder up via (1) Tier-A stock avatars now, (2) Tier-B viseme talking-head once a keyframe track lands on widgets, (3) integrate-via-import for Tier-C via Rive/Lottie exports from third-party tools (Adobe Character Animator, Cascadeur) so the rig itself lives outside the browser. Pairs with the keyframe-track foundation needed for Tier-B animation. Unblocks: "AI avatar of me" feature pillar; closes the gap to D-ID / Synthesia / HeyGen on the brand-video side. |
| 903 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **Audience-specific template seeds identified during landing-page evaluation — athlete recruit reel, musician EPK, realtor property tour, founder pitch, course / lesson trailer, conference talk teaser, testimonial montage, before / after transformation, dance reel, voice-over reel** (opened 2026-05-21 — surfaced evaluating new template types per audience) | Today's catalog ships 17 templates ([catalog.tsx:147-409](hired.video/frontend/components/studio/templates/catalog.tsx#L147-L409)) optimized for job-seeker / recruiter / employer / film personas. Underserved demographics with clear demand: (a) athletes — NCAA-style recruit reel with jersey # / position / stats lower-thirds + coach contact, (b) musicians — EPK (electronic press kit) with bio scene / track strip / venue list / booking CTA, (c) realtors — property tour with drone B-roll + room captions + agent CTA (paid demographic), (d) founders — YC-style 60s pitch (problem / what / traction / ask) distinct enough from `personal_brand` to deserve its own seed, (e) educators — course / lesson trailer (creator persona per [project_creator_persona_model](memory:project_creator_persona_model.md)), (f) speakers — conference talk teaser (reuses `tool_promo` shape), (g) B2B — testimonial montage (customer quotes cut together), (h) transformations — before / after (fitness / design / renovation), (i) dancers — full-body framing, music-bed required, beat-locked, (j) VO actors — audio-first, waveform viz, genre chips (overlaps with #894's `voice` mediaKind). Each new template = 1 `TEMPLATE_CATALOG` entry + 1 `template-seeds.ts` entry + 1 `audiences[]` tagging (#899) — no engine work, all compose existing scene / widget primitives. Defer until #900 landing-page conversion validates per-audience demand (don't over-build; ship audience pages with existing templates, watch CVR, then add the seed that closes the gap). |
| 928 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **"Animations / Animated Movies" splits into Phase 5 short loops (existing Timeline runtime) + a future `animated_film` canvas runtime for rigged character animation** (opened 2026-05-22 — customer feedback; corrected from "feature-length is out of scope" after #927 reframe) | Two tracks under the canvas-runtime extensibility model (PRD §1.2): (1) **Short animated loops on the existing Timeline runtime** — Phase 5 work already on the roadmap. `mediaKind: "animation"` enum + output profiles (GIF / WebP / APNG / transparent MP4) are defined; needs catalog entries (logo loop / loading spinner / reaction GIF / explainer loop) + a GIF/WebP encoder strategy (`gif.js` or ffmpeg.wasm via the existing render facade) + onion-skin overlay on the canvas. (2) **Future `animated_film` canvas runtime** — feature-length character animation (rigs / bones / IK / lip-sync / keyframe interpolation across hundreds of scenes) is a different RUNTIME, not a different scope. Plug into the same kind-dispatch slot as #927 (game) + #929 (interactive_comic) + #930 (CAD). `mediaKind: "animated_film"` would mount a rig-aware editor (`<RigStage/>` — Spine / DragonBones / Three.js skeleton), persist `rig_state` JSONB, render distributed via Web Workers or server-side ffmpeg + Blender headless. Sister-product split (`anim.video` or fold into hired.video proper) is the same decision as for game and CAD — keep open until the engine bundle bloats the main app. Action: ship (1) per the existing PRD Phase 5; (2) lands alongside the broader canvas-runtime-extensibility work in #931. The studio doesn't tell the customer "no" — it tells them "via this canvas, not the Timeline one." |
| 929 | T3 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `feature` | **`interactive_comic` canvas runtime + a near-term `comic_motion` Timeline template — TWO tracks, both supported** (opened 2026-05-22 — customer ask; corrected from "branching is out of scope" to "branching is a future canvas runtime" after #927 reframe) | Two tracks: (1) **Near-term linear comic-style video** — Timeline-runtime template `comic_motion` (mediaKind `video`) once the widget catalog gains `canvas-v2:speech-bubble` (rounded shape + tail + text) and `canvas-v2:panel-grid` (multi-image layout per scene). Reuses voiceover (a2) + sound effects (a1) tracks already in the spec. Output is a regular MP4 / vertical 9:16 for mobile reading. (2) **Future `interactive_comic` canvas runtime** (per PRD §1.2 canvas-runtime extensibility) — `mediaKind: "interactive_comic"` mounts a page-graph editor (`<ComicStage/>`), persists `comic_state` JSONB (pages + panels + choice nodes + branching edges), exports EPUB3 / CBR / hosted web reader bundle. Same dispatch plumbing as #927 (game) + #930 (CAD). Output profiles + publish destinations would be: EPUB3 file / CBR archive / web-embed iframe / hosted reader URL. Action: ship (1) first since it reuses existing Timeline runtime + only needs new widgets; (2) can land alongside the broader canvas-runtime-extensibility work in #931. Pairs with the audiobook + voice-acting work (Phase 4) — narrated comics share TTS + voice-clone plumbing. |
| 1116 | T4 | 🔲 Open | Later | P3 | `quality` | **Right-rail column stacks two `CollapsiblePanel`s vertically inside one flex-col with no inner overflow management — works today but adding a 3rd panel will pressure layout** (opened 2026-05-24 alongside #1111) | [`StudioEditorV2.tsx`](hired.video/frontend/pages/studio/v2/StudioEditorV2.tsx) now wraps the per-kind Properties panel + the new cross-studio AI panel in a `<div className="flex flex-col">` so they share the right column. The Properties panel gets `flex-1 overflow-y-auto`; the AI panel sits below it with default-closed state. Acceptable for 2 panels; a 3rd (e.g. a future Layers / Outline panel) would need an explicit shell that allocates fractional heights or supports a stacked-tab mode. Bundle with the next right-rail expansion. |
